#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cba1591f419653a58de73519f06b9c64b57e92b88376a7a3a1a22f62e45bc74.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/db/dbe82125589c07f379dc34f725c5052ebf2f50ace729a5294d749bc82e5f796c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff3e6c7ad9b4c8aa8e45f374a284c0f55a4655acab5ce77650e5e5eab8a3eea7.svg)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/7e/7e2e5d56fa414820a4a21b54c1c382e822da079044416e35d511f75a8b4a4a3f.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/69/69886ad4b83344964ea9341595b201aaf8725d805afc0b71036413708ecbe1bf.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/4d/4d63a240ce9979a10db9b4b339c5e66387f030b7434e6fe544d32b1a122cb94d.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f604278ad5afd886a82142b3511ca042a7a1568c2dabb901ae7fb117508c2953.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/54/54e0d9e7d7cd07271dfc6a6c3439a7c394e5bf3d85f0630dd1c6f9889561f2c0.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164" :question/problem "![](images/71/71ae5f07963d6fc4006ac682fb99d9654957564b0238494aa5d6f2c6ab3a1161.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/c0/c037ac599b0f26311ef49cea15a50b3c215f5f66af6677cc400470e36ac1dc12.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e8/e89397c2eb8ac9affa119178536b827fe4c9f710c5569dbdc5138c3d6498a413.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/cb/cbbbb9019a6fe09c63e1a69478321553144988117e35d9947336c964841b9cfc.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ea/ea9a9bf2fc7a7328202006234fa0288237b663ae31c382cc0e98f301eb3999ae.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2512f3a3-20fb-505e-aa3a-5251056c13ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269596} {:answer/id #uuid "574019c5-ee82-51f0-8bf1-fc528162b812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270039} {:answer/id #uuid "59682479-9091-54d7-94ac-8fcf8d47bfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270061} {:answer/id #uuid "77a78792-307d-52f7-b8e7-a3874f86b239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270329} {:answer/id #uuid "f5295862-adaf-5c67-9a31-fbdd0e3e18bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59682479-9091-54d7-94ac-8fcf8d47bfb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270061} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3714f5-a8f9-5d60-a0db-adf3ee87948c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb5619f-2165-5346-b048-85008466cdea" :question/math-academy-id "q-140780" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532bfb59-affa-5c62-a8e7-358e36a9bc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270008} {:answer/id #uuid "aea60c28-5e87-5191-952b-3b00f7cb6adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} {:answer/id #uuid "c179c9da-d404-5cbd-968a-2220b6fba57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270984} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3ecd7b-55a3-5ef3-ad46-ad378f716f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271197} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6dc910-3586-5cbb-930a-6f920df14dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea60c28-5e87-5191-952b-3b00f7cb6adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270821} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f87073-a16a-52a8-8a68-7c23a2ba2530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6cb0f9e-f73e-53e5-b7a2-f0d1aeb7047d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140825" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4122dbe8-2aa3-5fe0-8235-f8f88042f6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355243} {:answer/id #uuid "63b85b2c-fc6c-5711-ad3c-a175e001f222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355552} {:answer/id #uuid "b23ede5b-5547-56c9-95e5-4f00097db8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356234} {:answer/id #uuid "bb641c7b-a84a-5e55-b647-5fa1b6de9657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356303} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d2aede-13fc-5d85-84f9-9ce62b7984cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb641c7b-a84a-5e55-b647-5fa1b6de9657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356303} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed8a4b1-cfab-5341-9833-b15bf55c292f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0da0513-2306-5296-a63a-c968889ba8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93999" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answe