egin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{5}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\frac{12}{13}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3920505f-07e3-5de3-8592-aff4791e689d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355178} {:answer/id #uuid "4960703c-0192-5960-aa37-1bdbcecc6b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355312} {:answer/id #uuid "687265e3-5f60-544b-af43-366398d2cba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355587} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4729b8-a117-5863-8607-94f606b6829e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355769} {:answer/id #uuid "a40ba485-4998-5058-b716-d271d64f982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d4729b8-a117-5863-8607-94f606b6829e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355769} :answer-field/id #uuid "911aec86-abb7-5c2b-9643-d8fd160ae43b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f4314ce-c016-5a5b-a906-45de8df0762e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94008" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d49197c-8ea0-5037-8658-b8800ba11d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394882} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb14de4-81a2-5a22-9b22-a9c795e5d80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394883} {:answer/id #uuid "2d287e51-ff4c-5d64-aaf3-306e594af48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} {:answer/id #uuid "298cf117-73a5-5dba-8a49-4651e4dc4bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394885} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a2c500-01a4-5141-91a3-878c1f2e4f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d287e51-ff4c-5d64-aaf3-306e594af48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394884} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1f9db6-ad26-5de3-b141-6cab0e8d74e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc9e75e4-89e6-5c5c-b897-fce34f0588db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94026" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af7ba2c-f995-5cdb-b7ee-cabe8c1cbeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491506} {:answer/id #uuid "3e19570d-c86d-5aef-8ce6-081272d7624d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491576} {:answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} {:answer/id #uuid "b82e7bd9-f08e-549b-a5ee-19697ab81504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491773} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c9344d-89ec-5291-9170-aea2d82d9332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b134fbf7-d5b6-5f41-ab04-2a7e561d26d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14357fc-2106-5858-9b65-b92159c22d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8da40f3d-5d18-5923-96f9-268309e6cbba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94029" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349a333f-7c5a-5481-82b0-60cb63d7e507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491558} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3fb012-e655-54c0-b54f-afddf4c20499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491644} {:answer/id #uuid "94417bd7-eb48-5c39-9406-4f41780ccd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491707} {:answer/id #uuid "c96bbaba-0f0d-5501-965f-1c3ab78bf9d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491796} {:answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa236c72-29cd-50bc-b16a-514c5b7d0eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f366c1-707a-566c-ae94-1cc85de0d171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c26421-0b8a-5150-b5ca-ed9ed8ecdd75" :question/math-academy-id "q-94038" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6.$ Computing $A-(-6)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b137cc-58d8-54b3-8432-760f0c24ad5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404614} {:answer/id #uuid "8a282dc6-f613-5c49-b5b1-97ae8b91871d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404615} {:answer/id #uuid "ea78a723-5fcb-5124-a34d-7c785209ed96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404616} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8a0614-8f54-566a-a177-8dffc8e8215a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404617} {:answer/id #uuid "214fedb5-e346-584d-9ffc-111bf69f0cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "214fedb5-e346-584d-9ffc-111bf69f0cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b983f-90c9-5dfc-9769-8b7c27a8d13e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "976b5be4-8521-548d-a6b3-f77ad28ed1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94041" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f0e8db-19ca-5e1a-acef-433c78628572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364511} {:answer/id #uuid "1823a197-4084-5668-86ee-6f6631856c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} {:answer/id #uuid "f00dd1c5-3fcc-5dff-bacb-b531e82059c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364513} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d58908-b478-5a13-939f-a731e544060f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364514} {:answer/id #uuid "8cee7f48-d011-59dc-ac39-3664c1537063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1823a197-4084-5668-86ee-6f6631856c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c6748-dc18-5328-8608-350f3c43bf42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "987ecebc-d297-5962-b8d0-90f351ffca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94043" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56bc7cf9-dd1b-5316-add9-231be19d109f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364518} {:answer/id #uuid "b27a1670-2d5b-5bba-92a6-f0a17c0ce246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364519} {:answer/id #uuid "881bcb91-1f0d-5fd7-97a5-86876b601912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} {:answer/id #uuid "d84e1a54-ac78-5b2a-a104-f35ae3afdc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364521} {:answer/id #uuid "db75b88c-c492-572e-968f-86146a9098af" :